Date: Tue, 7 Dec 1993 20:37:24 -0800
From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Subject: Vinge, The Singularity, and Despair

At 11:00 AM 12/7/93 -0500, Edward J OConnell wrote:
>Vinge, The Singularity, and Runaway Intelligence
>
>I highly recommend that everyone run out and get a copy of the
>latest Whole Earth Review and read Vernor Vinge's article on
>the singularity. Nothing new, really, but seeing it all laid
>out so eloquently was...
>
>Nauseating.
>
>Runaway intelligence increase may give us the Jupiter Brains in
>our lifetime. Will the Jupiter brains give a rat's ass about
>us? If we choose to become Jupiter brains ourselves, to what
>degree can we be said to still exist? I don't remember being a
>blastula--a blastula wasn't *me.* I don't imagine a Jupiter
>Brain that started with my mind as its central core will think
>of itself as me, either.

I feel the same way -- I don't expect that "dV/dt Mark VII" will have more
than a flicker of nostalgia for the old "Dave Mark I"... perhaps enough to
keep the original skeleton around somewhere as a memento, or the original
skin as a lampshade <fearsome grin>.  I sure as hell don't think of myself
as "being" "the same as" the blastula I grew from, either.

However, unlike you, I don't see anything wrong with this.  Why the hell
should I be homesick for the old fallopian tubes?  I just don't see the
problem.  The first-grader that I used to be no longer exists, except as
fading recollections in the overcrowded memory banks of my meat brain. 
Should I mourn him, or instead cherish what he became?  I give him credit
for having had the seeds to become me (to quote Mark Miller), and I salute
the determination that carried him through all the self-transformations
that resulted in Dave(1993), but I'd rather be me than him.

Which enriches the Universe more:  a spacefaring complex of sophisticated
mental agencies and capabilities that can stride from star to star like a
god, reshaping planets at will?  Or the one-brained bag of contaminated
water sitting here typing these words at you?  Or the chicken egg I ate for
breakfast yesterday?  Is it worth the loss of the chicken egg to continue
the existence of "Dave Mark I"?  Is it worth the absorption of "Dave Mark
I" to create that Jupiter Brain?  I think so.

You make it sound as if there's some alternative.  There isn't.  We can't
preserve "Dave Mark I" as he exists today, because "Dave Mark I" is a
process, not an object.  (BONUS QUESTION:  Who was it who said "I seem to
be a verb"?)  Life is function, and function produces change.  It's
impossible to remain alive without learning, changing, growing; inevitably,
you end up either dead or as a Jupiter Brain.  You can slow down the
process of growth, and end up as a Jupiter Brain in 50 million years
instead of 50, but you'll still get there eventually, and that complex
creature simply won't cherish the identity of the present "you" as much as
the present "you" does.  There just isn't enough of the present "you" to
hold the attention of a Jupiter Brain.  Life goes on.

>Besides, I won't have the cash to become a Jupiter Brain.

I'm sure willingness will be much more the problem than cash -- can you
imagine Ross Perot uploading within his foreseeable shelf-life?  Nor can I.

>It seems to me most likely that my body--and brain-- will
>become Jupiter Brain food.

<salivates>

>If the Jupiter Brains are as amused
>by such notions as 'animal rights' as most list members, (seems
>likely) we will be scooped up in their cogitating jaws like
>planton by a sperm whale. With as much compassion as this list
>feels towards the snail darter.

Quite probably.  As a great sage said, "You're either part of the parade or
part of the road."  If I fell drowning into a stream peopled with snail
darters, would they recognize my suffering?  Would they feel bad about not
being able to help?  What, you say they're not capable of it?  Then why the
heck should I care about them if they're incapable of giving a fig about
me?

(If you mean caring about them for _practical_ reasons, such as their
ecological function, then I agree -- the biosphere is a sufficiently
complex machine, and sufficiently necessary to our survival, that we ought
not to throw away parts of it until we know what they do -- but it sounds
as though your snail darter concerns are more sentimental than practical.)
  
>This has really got me down. What is the use of cryonics?
>Runaway intelligence will have as much interest in reviving me,
>as I have of finding a Mac Plus to run an old typing tutor
>program I still have laying around on a floppy I've never
>gotten around to erasing.

Far be it from me to comment on your probable worth to uploaded beings of
the future.  But I have friends I care about, whom I would like to have
grow along with me.  I also have friends who care about me, and have
expressed the desire to have me around in future decades.  Presumably one
Jupiter Brain can build others.  My suggestion is to make yourself valuable
(liked, useful, loved, amusing, interesting) to people you feel are likely
to end up as Jupiter Brains (modesty forbids me to suggest where you might
find such folks), and to make sure you continue to grow as fast as they do,
so you stay interesting or amusing or whatever quality they value in you.

>Funny. The list, and our religious texts (selfish Gene,
>Drexler, Moravec, etc.) has convinced me of the eternal
>Darwinian struggle for all resources.

Yes, Jay, this is why your mother kept pushing you away from the table as a
child, so she could have more to eat.  This is why your parents spent their
income on mink coats and caviar for themselves during your childhood, while
you had no shoes.  This is why your friends charge you a dime to use their
bathroom every time you come over.  (HINT:  People share with people they
care about.)

>My naive belief in
>centuries of human scale intelligence and post scarcity, so
>that I might live a thousand years with a million friends
>creating freely all that I could ever dream to create--has
>vanished in the light of eternal free-market competition.

Somehow, trading that in for god-scale intelligence, so that I might live a
billion years with a billion friends, creating more than an entire
human-scale species might create in its entire lifetime, doesn't seem like
a letdown to me.  I just have enough self-confidence to imagine myself as
part of the parade, rather than part of the road, and to work toward it
instead of emitting victim noises.

>Runaway intelligence suggests that very soon, (20-50 years)
>there will be virtually nothing that I can contribute to the
>society of beings that rule this planet.  (Not that I'm
>contributing that much now. :-) )

That statement sounds quite dysthymic.  Have you always had this negative
estimation of yourself?  Don't you have any friends?  (BONUS QUESTION:  If
so, why?  If not, why not?)

Robert Heinlein was classified as "totally disabled" around 1940 or so. 
Oddly enough, fifty years later, millions of people (including many of us
on the list) consider that he changed and enriched their lives to a
significant degree.  What if Heinlein had predicted in 1940 that, 50 years
hence, he would have nothing to contribute?  (BONUS QUESTION:  What does
"Dynamic Optimism" mean?)

>To wit: We rent our atoms from the owners. The owners are the
>smartest beings around--always.

Strangely enough, despite a mercantilist system designed for that result,
the richest people in America do not yet own everything.  In fact, as Ross
Perot delights in pointing out, the personal fortunes of the entire Forbes
400 won't even cover one year's federal budget deficit.  Unless nanotech
comes about as the exclusive product of one or at most a handful of
individuals, it seems very unlikely to me that some elite will end up
owning everything.  (Even though my novel _Tin Gods and Carbon Angels_ is
based on just that premise.)  If you're really worried, you should be
agitating in favor of the Reverse Polish Moon Treaty.

>We, most of us anyway--(there
>may be some cut off. Lets say the millionaires on the list
>might get to upload.) will shortly be peripherialized, whether
>we admit it to ourselves or not. Cows and owls and snail
>darters may have illusions of autonomy, too.  They don't.
[Don't what?  Don't have any illusions, or don't have any autonomy?]

Let's say you could communicate with a cow (I'm stealing from Varley's
_Steel Beach_ here, which should go on the Extropian reading list ASAP). 
Let's say you could project it two images and determine which one its
little cow brain would cause it to walk towards.  Let's say you gave it a
choice between a life in which there was frequently drought, starvation,
disease, and painful, terrifying death at the jaws of coyotes or mountain
lions; and an alternative life in which a being of godlike power and
intelligence (my grandpa the cattle rancher) does everything in his power
to protect the herd from the above calamities, a life which ends with a
fast and painless (compared to being torn apart by coyotes) demise.  Which
do you think the cow would take?

This is a pretty silly question, because cows can't conceptualize about
higher abstractions like death, the future, autonomy, and choosing the
lesser of two evils.  They're about smart enough to distinguish between
suffering and contentment, and that's about it.

Now, (says "dV/dt Mark VII" to "Perry Mark IIX") let's suppose you could
communicate with a meat human.  Let's say you could transmit a measly
megabyte of text into his e-mail account and get back another megabyte that
he writes back to you.  Let's say you give him a choice between a life in
which there was frequently disease, starvation, frustration, murder,
mayhem, cannibalism, child molestation, and painful, terrifying death from
cancer or AIDS or a gunshot wound in the small intestine; and an
alternative life in which a being of godlike power and intelligence ("Perry
Mark IIX") does everything in his power to protect the tribe from the above
calamities, a life in which the human can exert himself to the limit of his
(natural) abilities, write songs and books and do calculus and whatever
else he feels like, a life which furthermore continues until the human
chooses to end it, except for a few folks who disappear mysteriously in the
night for reasons which are never quite adequately explained, but which is
considerably less alarming than being shot in the gut by a crazed crack
addict.  Which do you think the human would take?

This is a pretty silly question, because humans can't conceptualize about
higher abstractions like glafnir, flobak, neebnorb, and trisecting
Glaubner's dilemma in life-thought moral space.  They're about smart enough
to distinguish between an adult human who can do quantum mechanics and a
cow, and that's about it.

My only point here is that domestication might not be so bad, if Jupiter
Brains find some use for humans.  I would rather be governed by one
competent god than 250 million morons any day.  (I would prefer that the
god be me, however.)

Now, as to snail darters.  I have terrible news for you, Jay.  Last March,
when you had that sore throat, it was absolutely historic.  It was the
first self-aware bacterium.  They were building the equivalent of little
mud huts and earthenware dams in your tonsils by the time you first noticed
a redness at the back of your throat, and by the time you started to feel
sick they had the equivalent of textile mills and steam railroads. 
Unfortunately for them, your immune system was prejudiced against
streptococcal-Americans, and at night while you slept, your body's elite
shock troops, the White Berets, went in and wiped them all out down to the
last kindergartener.

Completely unnecessary, did I mention?  Their ethical philosophers had just
discovered environmentalism, and were already preaching that the activities
of strepkind might be damaging to the environment (you).  In another three
days they would have been completely switched over to non-polluting
industries and you would have felt fine.  However, you just didn't care. 
Their continued existence would have been only a minor inconvenience to
you, but you unknowingly went ahead and annihilated them.

Can you prove to me that the preceding _didn't_ happen?  Because I'm
claiming it did, and you should feel _terrible_ about it.  Even if they
weren't self-aware, they were a completely new species of bacteria, never
before seen, unlikely to ever exist again, and you wiped them out.  Don't
you feel just awful?

>Unless they [I assume "they" is the Jupiter Brains]
>are charmingly naive, muddle-headed
>environmentalists, Human scale intelligence won't be able to
>pay the rent on their bodies--or pay for the hardware to run
>their minds.

I am quite sure that some of them _will_ be charmingly naive, muddle-headed
environmentalists, and I'm sure they will build a "human preserve" for meat
people to live in.  (See "Let's say you could communicate with a human..."
above.)

>Vinge suggests we may end up incorporated, like
>agents, into a vast society of mind. Of course, we will have
>about as much free will as a mitochondria.

Assuming you believe in free will in the first place.  Let's say you could
communicate with a mitochondrion...

>[Musings on death & futility elided]
>Perhaps I'm in need of a little prozac.
>E. Jay O'Connell_____________________________________ejo@world.std.com

I'll say!  Jeez, Jay, lighten up!  These changes are going to happen so
fast, and there is so much work to be done, that anyone who even _knows_
about this stuff is so much further ahead of the pack, and will be in so
much demand, that your mere _knowledge of the existence_ of the Extropians
list will be enough to get you into Heaven -- er, I mean, into a Jupiter
Brain.  Really.  Trust me.
