Date: Tue, 7 Dec 1993 20:47:06 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: form, space and order

*
It's possible to think in multidimensional space that is not physical. 

Behavioral scientists make wide use of a loose umbrella called
"multidimensional scaling." It ranges from the very simple space spanned by
3 orthogonal unit vectors (cf., Osgood, Suci and Tannenbaum's "Semantic
Differential") to the wild n-space configurations in Riemann space used in
some areas of communication research (cf. Woelfel, _The Measurement of
Communication Processes_ for a discussion of "metric" multidimensional
scaling of many dimensions (10,11,... not uncommon). The latter is neat,
because vector algebra can be used on the eigenstructures to generate
messages which are marginally more persuasive to certain targeted audience
segments. 

Two general references for the curious are _Geometric Representations of
Relational Data_ by Lingoes, Roskam and Borg [yes, Borg] and _An
Introduction to Multidimensional Scaling_ by Schiffman, Reynolds and Young.
If I recall, these are all from Academic Press, but I'm unsure. 

Commercial marketers also think in n-space in both product design and
product positioning. An example is balancing various food product aspects
along independent dimensions such as ease of preparation, color, taste,
texture and weight. In positioning, it's typical to place messages
detailing attributes along dimensions of cost, utility, quality, gender and
status. Many, many attributes abound and can be incorporated into these
activities. Work with this stuff long enough, and the mind starts to
conceive of multidimensional space far afield from the physical analog.

The geometry gets most entertaining when the basic physical space
triangular inequalities are violated: there are those who prefer Coke over
Pepsi, Pepsi over RC Cola, and RC Cola over Coke. This is a regular
empirical occurrence. Much of the behavioral science MMDS work has been
driven by such occurences. Go figure.


