Subject: Paradox: Knowledge is Powerless

how can people know what is the best thing to do, and still, not do it? 

the ubiquitous examples of this paradox make it necessary to pick out 
particularly interesting e.g.: relaxation techniques have a proven 
positive impact on health and cognition, yet most people who want to 
improve either their health or minds would assent to the importance of 
relaxation, and in the next breath, look for ways around this simple 
task. 

Two others: 1) Goal setting may be even more powerful than literacy as a 
technique for self-development, yet, it is not nearly as widely 
practiced as it is praised. 

2) Holding to a positive outlook, until one has been utterly routed, can 
be demonstrated to be more conducive to health and cognitive vigor. 
Knowing this fact is not in itself capable of persuading pessimists.

so what causes people to adopt new beliefs? this question goes to the 
very core of how learning occurs, placing it in a developmental frame of 
reference. My hunch is that knowledge, without motivation, is powerless. 

if one examines the progress of the sciences, the essential commitment 
has been to a further elaboration of knowledge. Yes, knowledge 
(eventually) transforms life practice, although most people view the 
entire scientific enterprise as a guide for avoiding Bad cholesterol.

If science had a marketing division, and attention were paid to 
motivation as well as content, the possibility for delivering 
information could be radically increased. 

Consider the Enzyme: can we find means of increasing the uptake of 
information, the use of available knowledge, in a much speedier fashion 
than occurs by simply publishing it?

Do we understand why people don't do what they know is good for them? Is 
the attempt to "understand" this problem just an iteration of 
intellectualization instead of actualization?

